 "notes": "Fixtures and expected labels were written by the same author as the stage skills, before this run, and were not changed after it.\n\nPlain Claude scored 1.0 on three of the six sets (interviews, profiles, pain logs). The task text spells out the definitions, and on these synthetic items that is enough. Those sets have no headroom, so every subject on them can only tie or lose; they need harder or more ambiguous items before they can show a win.\n\nThe three wins rest on thin ground. The stage 0 card and skill won only on 'riskiest', the most judgment-heavy label in the fixtures: they got as-1 and as-4 right where plain Claude did not, and lost as-5 (picked the pricing assumption e4 over client acceptance e3). The stage 1 skill won on one item, au-2, where plain Claude and the audience-first card both called a reachable Lisbon bookkeeper pin not narrower.\n\nThe only miss by the Mom Test auditor and the stage 3 skill was int-5 in one repeat: they counted the closing turn (a booked screen-share) as a second fact and gave pass instead of flag. The Laya earlyvangelist arm marked has_budget false on prof-4, where the notes say the school has a software budget; the question set asks whether the person spends on this problem, so the label there is arguable.\n\nThe two errored calls are stage 3 skill calls on int-8. The API refused both with a usage-limit error at the end of the run (the API key's limit was reached; it resets 2026-10-01). They are counted and left out of the scores. The +laya subjects used a local Laya 0.3.20 (english and multilingual checkpoints) behind a throwaway /v1/systemone wrapper around Router.predict; no subject's verdict differed with Laya on or off.",
